  "content": "<h2>What Supervisor-Worker Architecture Actually Means in Production</h2><p>The term 'multi-agent orchestration' gets used loosely. In practice, most systems described this way are either single agents with tool access or flat collections of agents that share a message queue. Neither handles real production workloads well. Supervisor-worker architecture is a specific design pattern where a dedicated orchestration layer — the supervisor — decomposes tasks, routes subtasks to specialised worker agents, aggregates results, and manages failure recovery. The supervisor holds state. Workers are stateless and replaceable.</p><p>This distinction matters when you move from demos to production. A flat agent topology works fine for a single-user proof of concept. It breaks under concurrent load, produces inconsistent outputs when agents disagree, and has no coherent recovery path when a worker fails mid-task. Supervisor-worker solves each of these problems, but only if the architecture is designed correctly from the start.</p>

<h2>The Supervisor Layer: What It Actually Does</h2><p>The supervisor is not a router. Routing — sending a message to the right queue — is a solved problem and doesn't require an LLM. The supervisor's job is task decomposition, dependency tracking, result synthesis, and failure arbitration. These require reasoning, which is why the supervisor is typically the most capable model in the system.</p><p>In a production deployment on Kubernetes, the supervisor runs as a dedicated pod with its own resource allocation, separate from worker pods. It maintains a task graph in Postgres — not in memory — so that a pod restart doesn't lose orchestration state. Each node in the task graph records the subtask definition, the assigned worker agent, the current status, the output, and any retry metadata. The supervisor polls this graph, not an in-memory queue, when deciding what to do next.</p>

<h3>Task Decomposition Patterns</h3><p>Supervisors decompose tasks in one of three patterns depending on the workload:</p><ul><li><strong>Sequential chains:</strong> Each subtask depends on the output of the previous. Used when later steps require information produced by earlier steps — for example, research followed by synthesis followed by formatting.</li><li><strong>Parallel fan-out:</strong> Independent subtasks dispatched simultaneously to multiple workers. Used when subtasks are genuinely independent — for example, scraping multiple sources concurrently. Fan-out is where Kafka earns its place: the supervisor publishes to multiple partitions, workers consume independently, and back-pressure is handled at the broker level rather than in application code.</li><li><strong>Conditional branching:</strong> The supervisor evaluates intermediate results and chooses a path. Used when the correct next step depends on what was found — for example, routing to a different worker if a data source returns empty results.</li></ul><p>Most real workloads combine all three. A task might fan out to five workers, collect results sequentially, then branch based on what comes back. The supervisor's task graph needs to express this without becoming unreadable. We use a directed acyclic graph (DAG) representation stored in Postgres with a recursive CTE for traversal. The schema is simple: nodes table with status and output columns, edges table with dependency type. The supervisor queries pending nodes whose dependencies are all in a completed state.</p>

<h2>Worker Agent Design: Stateless by Constraint, Not Convention</h2><p>Workers should be stateless. Not as an aspiration — as a hard architectural constraint enforced by the deployment model. A worker pod that accumulates state between tasks becomes a reliability liability: it behaves differently depending on what ran before, it can't be scaled horizontally without coordination, and its failure takes task history with it.</p><p>Enforcing statelessness means workers receive everything they need in the task payload. No worker should need to query a shared cache to understand its context. The supervisor constructs the full context — including relevant outputs from upstream tasks — and includes it in the Kafka message. Workers read, execute, write output to Postgres, and acknowledge the message. That's the complete lifecycle.</p><p>In Kubernetes terms, workers run as Deployments (not StatefulSets) with horizontal pod autoscaling based on Kafka consumer group lag. When a topic partition accumulates lag — meaning tasks are queuing faster than workers consume them — the HPA scales up worker replicas. When lag clears, it scales down. This is clean, observable, and doesn't require custom autoscaling logic.</p>

<h3>Worker Specialisation vs. Generalist Workers</h3><p>A common design question: should workers be specialised by task type, or should a single worker type handle all tasks? The answer depends on the variance in resource requirements across task types.</p><p>If all tasks have similar compute and time profiles, a single worker type simplifies deployment and maximises resource utilisation. If task types vary significantly — some tasks call external APIs and complete in milliseconds, others run local inference and take seconds — specialised workers let you right-size resource requests per task type. Mixing them in a single worker pool means either over-provisioning for fast tasks or under-provisioning for slow ones.</p><p>In practice, we run specialised worker Deployments per task category, each consuming from its own Kafka topic. The supervisor routes to the correct topic based on task type. This adds a small amount of operational complexity (more Deployments to manage) but significantly improves resource efficiency and makes per-task-type scaling independent.</p>

<h2>Kafka as the Coordination Layer</h2><p>The choice of Kafka over a simpler queue (Redis, RabbitMQ, SQS) is deliberate and worth explaining. The primary reasons are durability, consumer group semantics, and replay capability.</p><p>Durability: Kafka persists messages to disk. If a worker pod crashes mid-processing, the message is not lost — it remains in the partition until explicitly acknowledged. Combined with Kafka's at-least-once delivery guarantee and idempotent worker design, this gives you reliable task execution without a separate dead-letter queue for every failure mode.</p><p>Consumer group semantics: Kafka's consumer group model maps cleanly to the worker pool pattern. Each worker type has its own consumer group. Adding a worker pod automatically joins it to the group and receives a partition assignment. Removing a pod triggers a rebalance. The broker handles coordination; the application doesn't need to.</p><p>Replay: When you need to reprocess tasks — because a worker had a bug, because a model was updated, because an upstream data source changed — Kafka lets you reset consumer group offsets and replay from a specific point. This is operationally invaluable and not available in queue systems that delete messages on acknowledgement.</p><p>The trade-off is operational overhead. Kafka requires a broker cluster, topic management, and monitoring of consumer group lag. For small workloads or early-stage deployments, this overhead isn't justified. We use Kafka when the workload has either high throughput requirements, replay requirements, or both. For lighter workloads, Postgres-backed queues (using SKIP LOCKED) are simpler and sufficient.</p>

<h2>State Management in Postgres</h2><p>The orchestration state — task graph, agent assignments, intermediate outputs, retry counts — lives in Postgres. Not in Redis, not in the supervisor's memory, not in a document store. Postgres gives you ACID transactions, which matter when the supervisor is updating task status and dispatching the next subtask atomically. Without transactional guarantees, you get race conditions: two supervisor instances both read a task as pending and both dispatch it.</p><p>The schema pattern we use separates orchestration state from task outputs. The orchestration tables (task graph nodes and edges, run metadata) are normalised and indexed for the supervisor's query patterns. Task outputs are stored as JSONB in a separate table, with the node ID as the foreign key. This keeps orchestration queries fast even as output data grows large.</p><p>For long-running deployments, task output tables grow without bound. We partition by run date and drop old partitions on a schedule. The retention period depends on whether outputs need to be auditable — regulated clients typically require 90-day retention; others can drop after 7 days.</p>

<h3>Handling Partial Failures</h3><p>Partial failure — where some workers in a fan-out succeed and others fail — is the hardest case to handle correctly. The naive approach is to fail the entire run and retry from scratch. This is safe but expensive: you re-execute work that already succeeded.</p><p>The correct approach is to retry only failed nodes. The supervisor identifies failed nodes (status = 'failed', retry_count < max_retries), resets their status to 'pending', and dispatches them again. Succeeded nodes are not re-executed. This requires id
